The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
rq = blk_get_request(q, in_len ? WRITE : READ, __GFP_WAIT);
if (IS_ERR(rq)) {
    err = PTR_ERR(rq);
    goto error;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code does not check if the `rq` pointer is valid before calling `blk_put_request(rq)` in the error handling path. If `blk_get_request` fails, `rq` will be an error pointer, and dereferencing it in `blk_put_request(rq)` can lead to crashes or undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (IS_ERR(rq)) {
    err = PTR_ERR(rq);
    goto error_free_buffer;
}
```
Reason:
The fix changes the error handling logic to jump to `error_free_buffer` instead of `error` when `rq` is an error pointer. This prevents the invalid pointer from being dereferenced by ensuring that `blk_put_request(rq)` is only called when `rq` is valid.

